===============================
CSS POSITIONING - NOTES
===============================

WHAT IS POSITIONING IN CSS?

Positioning in CSS defines how an element is placed in the webpage layout.

By default, all HTML elements follow normal document flow:
- Block elements stack vertically
- Inline elements appear side by side

The "position" property changes how an element is placed.


SYNTAX:

selector {
    position: value;
}


TYPES OF POSITION IN CSS:

1. static
2. relative
3. absolute
4. fixed
5. sticky


================================
1) position: static
================================

WHAT IS STATIC?

- Static is the default value of the position property.
- Every HTML element is static by default.
- If we do not specify position, it automatically uses:
  position: static;


KEY CHARACTERISTICS:

- Default positioning
- Follows normal document flow
- Cannot be moved using top, left, right, or bottom
- z-index does not work
- No overlapping


IMPORTANT RULE:

If an element has:

position: static;

Then these properties will NOT work:

top
left
right
bottom
z-index


EXAMPLE:

HTML:

<div class="box1">Box 1</div>
<div class="box2">Box 2</div>


CSS:

.box1 {
    width: 150px;
    height: 150px;
    background-color: lightblue;
}

.box2 {
    width: 150px;
    height: 150px;
    background-color: lightcoral;
    position: static;
    top: 50px;   /* This will NOT work */
}


OUTPUT BEHAVIOR:

- Box 1 appears first
- Box 2 appears below it
- top: 50px is ignored
- Layout remains normal


WHAT IS NORMAL DOCUMENT FLOW?

Normal flow means:
- Block elements appear one below another
- Inline elements appear side by side
- Elements do not overlap
- No manual movement happens


WHEN DO WE USE STATIC?

- We usually do not manually use static.
- It is mainly used to reset positioning.

